From: shanshan <samsom0409@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 19 Oct 2026 03:00:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] w4a8: declare only the experts a MoE call can route to

`npu_grouped_matmul` charges for every expert the weight tensor declares, not
for the ones the group list activates. On one TP16 `down` shard, K=128 N=6144,
eight active experts of one row each, a call that declares all 256 costs
76.644 us and one that declares eight costs 31.240.

The saving is in the declaration rather than in the bytes. A three-arm control
holding the allocation, the bytes read and the reuse distance fixed, and varying
only the declared count, puts the declaration effect at 41.511 us on ND weights
with read locality separated out as a further 0.8 to 9.2 us. Counting only the
declaration and charging the gather in full, the net is 26.3 us per layer; the
measured end to end is 76.644 against 29.530 plus a 15.190 us gather, so 31.9 us
per layer, and at roughly 75 built-in grouped calls in a round that is 2.4 ms.

The compaction is device-side and static-shaped so it survives graph capture.
`topk` over the counts returns `active` slots, which cover every nonzero one
whenever `active` is at least the number of experts a call can route to, and
sorting them ascending preserves the row-to-expert order the operator walks.
Nothing is read back to the host, so no synchronisation is introduced into the
decode stream.

Two things this does not do, with the reasons.

It does not take the cheaper path of narrowing a view when the active experts
happen to be contiguous, even though the operator accepts such a view, keeps it
in FRACTAL_NZ and returns bit-identical output. Choosing that path means testing
the group list's contents, and inside a captured graph that test is a device to
host read. The view path stays available to eager callers and would be free
under a routing policy that guaranteed contiguity, which is a model-quality
change and out of scope.

It requires w2 to be left in ND. `index_select` on a FRACTAL_NZ weight fails
inside TransData, a per-expert `copy_` into an NZ destination fails the same
way, and so does casting back out of NZ, so the gather is not expressible on the
tensor `maybe_trans_nz` produces. Leaving w2 in ND is not a cost: at full
declaration on this shape ND measures 71.041 us against NZ's 76.644, so it is
5.603 us per call faster on its own. The NZ conversion for w13 is untouched.

Verified bit-exact on real checkpoint weights, not on synthesised ones. Real
`down_proj` tensors for eight experts of layer 3 out of
/workspace/models/GLM-5.2-w4a8c8 -- (3072, 2048) int8, 6144 float32 scales,
(6144, 16) correction, four bits -- placed in the production raw layout and put
through the real `process_weights_after_loading`. The compacted call and the
full-bank call agree on every one of the 49,152 output elements, maximum
absolute difference 0.0.

The rows beyond `sum(group_list)` were checked explicitly rather than assumed,
because an active-row comparison would hide a difference there and a combine
step that indexed by global expert id would see it: with eight live rows and
eight dead tail rows, both arms leave the tail all zero and bit-identical. The
operator mutates its activation in place -- up to 251 on the int8 input -- and
mutates it identically in both arms; the per-token scale is untouched.

Limits of the figures. The per-call numbers are from a single die with
neighbouring tenants present, timed inside captured graphs because this
operator's eager host enqueue is 28.6 us against 59.1 us of device time and an
event pair around eager calls can read the host rather than the kernel; the
round figure multiplies them by a family count taken from a 16-rank profile.
The gather moves 12.6 MB at 15.19 us, which is 824 GB/s against the die's
1540 GB/s marginal read rate, so roughly 7 us per layer of headroom remains in
the copy itself. Dividing its bytes by peak bandwidth would have predicted
4.1 us and been optimistic by 3.7 times.

`apply_gmm1`'s built-in path is not the one in use -- the fused
`grouped_matmul_swiglu_quant_v2` serves `gate_up` -- but the same change applies
there if it is ever enabled, worth 10.5 us per layer at that shape.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/vllm_ascend/quantization/methods/w4a8/w4a8.py
+++ b/vllm_ascend/quantization/methods/w4a8/w4a8.py
@@ -115,6 +115,59 @@
     )
 
 
+
+# The built-in grouped matmul charges for every expert the weight tensor
+# declares, not for the ones the group list activates. Setting this False
+# restores the previous behaviour; see _compact_expert_declaration.
+_COMPACT_EXPERT_DECLARATION = True
+# ACL's ND format code. vllm_ascend names only ACL_FORMAT_FRACTAL_NZ, so this
+# one is spelled out rather than imported.
+_ACL_FORMAT_ND = 2
+
+
+def _compact_expert_declaration(layer, weight, scale, bias, group_list, active):
+    """Hand the grouped matmul only as many experts as this call can use.
+
+    At 256 declared experts against eight active, one TP16 `down` call costs
+    76.6 us; declaring eight costs 31.2. The saving is in the declaration, not
+    in the bytes: a three-arm control holding the allocation, the bytes read and
+    the reuse distance fixed, and varying only the declared count, puts it at
+    41.5 us with read locality separated out.
+
+    The compaction is device-side and static-shaped so it survives graph
+    capture. `topk` over the counts returns `active` slots, which necessarily
+    cover every nonzero one when `active` is at least the number of experts a
+    call can route to, and sorting them ascending preserves the row-to-expert
+    order the operator walks. Nothing is read back to the host, so no
+    synchronisation is introduced.
+
+    Returns its arguments unchanged unless every weight is ND. `index_select`
+    on a FRACTAL_NZ weight fails inside TransData, and so does casting back out
+    of one, so this route requires w2 to be left in ND at load time -- which is
+    also 5.6 us per call faster than NZ at full declaration on the decode shape.
+    """
+    if not _COMPACT_EXPERT_DECLARATION or active >= group_list.shape[0]:
+        return weight, scale, bias, group_list
+    if any(torch_npu.get_npu_format(tensor) != _ACL_FORMAT_ND
+           for tensor in weight):
+        return weight, scale, bias, group_list
+    index = torch.sort(torch.topk(group_list, active).indices).values
+    buffers = getattr(layer, "_compact_expert_buffers", None)
+    if buffers is None or buffers["index"].shape != index.shape:
+        buffers = {"index": torch.empty_like(index),
+                   "group_list": torch.empty_like(group_list[:active]),
+                   "weight": [tensor[:active].clone() for tensor in weight],
+                   "scale": [tensor[:active].clone() for tensor in scale],
+                   "bias": [tensor[:active].clone() for tensor in bias]}
+        layer._compact_expert_buffers = buffers
+    for name, group in (("weight", weight), ("scale", scale), ("bias", bias)):
+        for position, tensor in enumerate(group):
+            torch.index_select(tensor, 0, index, out=buffers[name][position])
+    torch.index_select(group_list, 0, index, out=buffers["group_list"])
+    return (buffers["weight"], buffers["scale"], buffers["bias"],
+            buffers["group_list"])
+
+
 @register_scheme("W4A8_DYNAMIC", "moe")
 class AscendW4A8DynamicFusedMoEMethod(AscendMoEScheme):
     """FusedMoE method for Ascend W4A8_DYNAMIC.
@@ -376,7 +429,11 @@
         else:
             self.process_weights_after_loading_modelslim(layer)
         layer.w13_weight.data = maybe_trans_nz(layer.w13_weight.data)
-        layer.w2_weight.data = maybe_trans_nz(layer.w2_weight.data)
+        if not _COMPACT_EXPERT_DECLARATION:
+            # The expert compaction gathers w2 with index_select, which fails
+            # inside TransData on a FRACTAL_NZ weight; ND is also 5.603 us per
+            # call faster at full declaration on the decode shape.
+            layer.w2_weight.data = maybe_trans_nz(layer.w2_weight.data)
         tensor_names = (
             "w13_weight",
             "w2_weight",
@@ -637,9 +694,14 @@
         return torch_npu.npu_dynamic_quant(hidden_states, dst_type=torch.int8)
 
     def apply_gmm2(self, mlp_compute_input: MoEMlpComputeInput, hidden_states, act_out_scale):
-        _, _, w2, w2_scale = self._get_mlp_weights(mlp_compute_input.layer)
-        _, bias2 = self._get_mlp_scale_bias(mlp_compute_input.layer)
+        layer = mlp_compute_input.layer
+        _, _, w2, w2_scale = self._get_mlp_weights(layer)
+        _, bias2 = self._get_mlp_scale_bias(layer)
         group_list, group_list_type = self._maybe_convert_group_list(mlp_compute_input)
+        if group_list_type == 1:
+            w2, w2_scale, bias2, group_list = _compact_expert_declaration(
+                layer, w2, w2_scale, bias2, group_list,
+                hidden_states.shape[0])
         return torch_npu.npu_grouped_matmul(
             x=[hidden_states],
             weight=w2,
